# TLS for the services on this instance. Caddy runs with --network host, so it
# binds 80/443 directly and reaches each backend on loopback. Every proxied request
# therefore arrives at the backend from 127.0.0.1, which is exactly the set
# internal/api/routes.go pins TrustedProxies to. Widening one without the other
# breaks the demo rate limiter.
#
# No ACME email, so Let's Encrypt cannot send expiry warnings. Caddy renews on its
# own; adding `email you@example.com` in a global block turns the warnings on.

# sslip.io resolves any IP embedded in the hostname, so this gets a real
# certificate without waiting on a DNS record. Swapping it for a vanity subdomain
# is this one line plus an A record; nothing else here changes.
52-22-210-61.sslip.io {
	reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:8080
}

# titlerate-java will listen on 127.0.0.1:8081. Left commented until it is actually
# deployed, because a site block pointing at a dead port answers 502, and a public
# URL that returns 502 is worse than one that does not exist yet.
#
# 52-22-210-61.sslip.io would collide with the block above, so titlerate needs its
# own hostname: a subdomain once DNS exists, or a second instance address.
